SOAPBOX: It's the first time I've operated in this contest for ten
SOAPBOX: years and it was really nice to be back running PSK63
SOAPBOX: again.  I only intended to give away a few points but I
SOAPBOX: lost track of time and so have made a full entry. 
SOAPBOX: Unfortunately I have no aerial for 80m so was limited to
SOAPBOX: 40m through 10m.
SOAPBOX: Lots of activity although nothing from north or south
SOAPBOX: America.  Have they forgotten that the PSK modes exist?
SOAPBOX: It was nice to only be able to run 50W as it means I could
SOAPBOX: save the energy costs!  Although, looking at some signals on
SOAPBOX: the waterfall, some were either running significantly more
SOAPBOX: or were seriously overdriving their radios.  Or both, more
SOAPBOX: than 50W and running high ALC.  But whatever.
SOAPBOX: There were some odd macro sets being run.  I was called by
SOAPBOX: a lot of EA stations who gave their province code in their
SOAPBOX: initial reply and a couple who didn't even send their callsigns.
SOAPBOX: I had to prompt them to get their callsign.
SOAPBOX: 20m was good, 15m and 10m nowhere near as busy as I expected.
SOAPBOX: I noticed strange varying in signals on 40m and suspect I've
SOAPBOX: logged quite a few errors on that band.
SOAPBOX: Overall, an enjoyable way to spend a weekend.  Thanks to
SOAPBOX: everyone for the points and thanks to the organisers.
SOAPBOX: Equipment here:  Kenwood TS-890, 50W to a Hexbeam and using
SOAPBOX: fldigi to log.  Totally unassisted, most Qs were from CQing.
SOAPBOX:
SOAPBOX: Please note, RD4HP sent the same serial number (030) to both
SOAPBOX: myself and 2E0INN.
